In this lesson we'll look at the area of a rectangle, which is just the amount of flat surface it covers, measured in square units like centimetres squared or metres squared. Here's the big idea. Imagine a rectangle that's four centimetres long and three centimetres wide. You could tile it with little one centimetre squares. There would be four squares along the length and three along the width, giving you twelve squares in total. That counting gives us the formula. The area of a rectangle equals length times width, as long as both measurements are in the same unit. So if someone tells you a rectangle is two metres by fifty centimetres, you have to convert first. Fifty centimetres is zero point five of a metre, and then two times zero point five gives you one square metre. The whole point of the formula is speed. Without it, counting squares on a big rectangle would take forever, but with it, the answer is instant. Now let's see it in action.